    body: ["The Delhi Sultanate 55\nIGNITE YOUR MIND\nIn your opinion, did the Turkish conquest had only a negative impact on the political landscape of the Ganga Valley, \nBihar, and Bengal, or did it also lead to the consolidation of regional power, the emergence of new political formations, \nand the development of administrative structures?\nINTRODUCTION TO THE DELHI SULTANATE\nDelhi Sultantate Period (c.1206-1526 CE)\nKhalji\n(c.1290-1320CE)\nSlave/Ilbari \n(c.1206-1290 CE)\nTughlaq\n(c.1320-1414CE)\nSaiyyad\n(c.1414-1451CE)\nLodhis\n(c.1451-1526CE)\nFig. 4.1: Delhi Sultanate Period\nFounding and Expansion\nThe Delhi Sultanate, marking a pivotal era in Indian \nhistory, was established between 1206 and 1526 CE. It \nemerged following the invasions of Mahmud of Ghazni \nand Muhammad Ghori, setting the stage for Muslim rule \nin the region. This period was governed by five dynasties: \nThe Slave, Khalji, Tughlaq, Sayyid, and Lodhi. Each dynasty, \noriginating from Turkish and Afghan backgrounds, uniquely \ninfluenced the political, social, and cultural landscape of \nNorth India and beyond.\nTransformation and Cultural Integration\nUnder these dynasties, the Sultanate expanded significantly, \nconsolidating power in North India, including regions like \nMalwa and Gujarat, and extending into the Deccan and \nSouth India. This era was characterized by profound societal \ntransformations, innovative administrative reforms, and \na fusion of cultural influences that reshaped the Indian \nsubcontinent.\nThe Slave Dynasty\nSlave Dynasty \n(1206 to 1290 CE)\nThe Qutbi \ndynasty (Qutb￾ud-din Aibak)\nSecond Ilbari \ndynasty \n(Balban)\nFirst Ilbari \ndynasty \n(Iltutmish)\nFig. 4.2: Slave Dynasty (1206-1290 CE)\nEstablishment and Phases\nThe Slave Dynasty, or the Mameluk/Ilbari Dynasty, was the \nfirst of the Sultanate’s ruling dynasties, spanning from 1206 \nto 1290 CE. The term ‘Mameluk’ reflects the rulers’ origins \nas slaves or military servants of Turkish descent. This dynasty \nwas foundational in establishing Muslim rule and setting \nadministrative and cultural norms in India.\nFig. 4.3: Slave Dynasty Map\nContributions and Leadership\nThe Slave Dynasty is recognized for its three distinct phases:\n The Qutbi Dynasty: Founded by Qutb-ud-din Aibak, it \nmarked the beginning of Muslim rule in India.\n The First Ilbari Dynasty: Established by Iltutmish, it \nconsolidated the Sultanate’s power and expanded its \ncultural influence."]
