    body: ["56 Medieval India \n The Second Ilbari Dynasty: Under Balban’s leadership, it \nfurther strengthened the Sultanate’s administrative and \nmilitary systems.\nEach phase contributed significantly to the governance, \nmilitary strength, cultural development, and architectural \nheritage of the Sultanate.\nQutb-ud-din Aibak (1206-1210 CE)\n Foundation of the Slave \nDynasty: Qutb-ud-din Aibak \nis revered as the founder of \nthe Slave Dynasty and is often \nregarded as the real founder \nof Muslim rule in India. He was \na Turkish slave of Muhammad \nGhori and played a pivotal role \nin the expansion of the Turkish \nSultanate in India following the \nBattle of Tarain.\n• Lakh Baksh: Aibak was popularly known as “Lakh \nBaksh,” which means ‘Giver of Lakhs (hundreds of \nthousands)’. This nickname was a testament to his \ngenerosity and his patronage of construction and \ncharitable works.\n Consolidation of Power: Following Ghori’s death in 1206 \nCE, Aibak faced challenges from various regional rulers \nand revolts but managed to establish his authority by \nFig. 4.4: Qutub-Ud-Din \nAibak\ndefeating them and pacifying rebellions. He severed ties \nwith Ghazni and established the Slave Dynasty as an \nindependent entity.\n Capital and Administration: He made Lahore his capital \nand assumed the title of ‘Sultan’. His administrative \npolicies laid the groundwork for the Sultanate’s future \ngovernance structure.\n Architectural and Cultural Contributions: Aibak \ninitiated the construction of the Qutub Minar and built \nthe Quwwat-ul-Islam (Might of Islam) Mosque in the \nMehrauli region of Delhi and Arhai din ka Jhonpra in \nAjmer. He was a patron of scholars like Hasan Nizami \nand Fakhr-ud-din.\n Demise: Qutb-ud-din Aibak’s reign was cut short by his \nsudden death in 1210 CE while playing chaugan (a game \nsimilar to modern-day polo), which was a popular royal \npastime.\nContinuing the Legacy\nAfter Aibak, the Slave Dynasty saw further consolidation \nand expansion under rulers like Iltutmish and Balban, each \nleaving a distinct imprint on the Sultanate’s history.\nThe initial phase of the Delhi Sultanate under the Slave \nDynasty set a precedent in terms of governance, cultural \nassimilation, and architectural innovation, creating a legacy \nthat would influence the Indian subcontinent for centuries \nto come.\nFig. 4.5: The Rulers of Slave Dynasty\nAram Shah (c. 1210 CE)\n Brief Reign and Inefficacy:\n• Succession and Opposition: Aram Shah, the son \nof Qutb-ud-din Aibak, succeeded his father to the \nthrone in 1210 CE. However, his reign was brief and \nmarked by incompetence. He was widely regarded \nas an incapable ruler, lacking the qualities needed to \ngovern effectively.\n• Rejection by Turkish Amirs: His ineffectiveness as a \nruler led to opposition from the Turkish amirs, the \nmilitary and administrative elite of the Sultanate. His \nrule lasted for only about eight months, during which \nhe failed to establish authority or gain the confidence \nof his subjects and peers.\n Overthrow and Replacement:\n• Invitation to Iltutmish: The Turkish chiefs of Delhi, \ndissatisfied with Aram Shah’s leadership, invited \nIltutmish, the governor of Badayun and son-in￾law of Qutb-ud-din Aibak, to claim the throne. \nIltutmish, who had both the military prowess and \nthe administrative acumen, was seen as a fitting \nalternative to lead the Sultanate."]
