    subtitle: "मूळ PDF मधील पूर्ण पृष्ठीय मजकूर",
    body: ["The Delhi Sultanate 57\n• Iltutmish’s Ascension: Iltutmish moved against \nAram Shah and successfully deposed him, becoming \nthe Sultan under the name of Shamsuddin. His \nascension marked the beginning of a more stable \nand consolidated phase in the history of the Delhi \nSultanate.\nIltutmish (1210-1236 CE)\n Consolidation and Administration:\n• Real Architect of the Sultanate: Iltutmish is often \nregarded as the real consolidator of Turkish rule in \nIndia. He belonged to the Ilbari tribe, leading to his \ndynasty being known as the Ilbari dynasty.\n• Early Life and Rise: Sold into slavery by his half￾brothers, Iltutmish was eventually purchased by \nAibak, who later became his father-in-law. This \nfamilial bond positioned Iltutmish favourably within \nthe hierarchy of the Sultanate.\n• Defiance and Threats: The initial years of his reign \nwere focused on securing his throne against internal \nrivals and external threats. Notable adversaries \nincluded commanders of Muhammad Ghori and \nindependent regional powers, alongside the looming \nthreat of the Mongols under Chengiz Khan.\n Military Campaigns and Diplomacy:\n• Battles and Conquests: Iltutmish’s military campaigns \nwere significant. He defeated Yaldauz in 1215 CE at \nthe Battle of Tarain and drove away Qabacha from \nPunjab in 1217 CE.\n• Mongol Threat and Diplomacy: With the Mongol \ninvasion led by Chengiz Khan threatening the \nnorthWestern frontier, Iltutmish displayed \nremarkable diplomatic skills. \n He tactfully refused asylum to fugitive khwarezm\nprince Jalaluddin Mangbarani, thereby averting a \ndirect Mongol assault on the Sultanate.\n• Expansion and Suppression of Revolts: From 1225 \nCE, Iltutmish focused on quelling disturbances in \nthe East. He successfully brought Bengal and Bihar\nback under the Sultanate’s control and launched \ncampaigns against Rajput chiefs, capturing significant \nterritories.\n Administrative Innovations and Cultural Contributions:\n• Iqta System: Iltutmish introduced the Iqta system, \nassigning land in lieu of salary to military officials \n(Iqtadars), who were responsible for maintaining law \nand order and collecting revenue.\n• Turkan-i-Chahalgani: He organized the Turkan-i￾Chahalgani, a group of forty powerful military leaders \nwho played a crucial role in the administration but \nlater assumed significant political power.\n• Recognition and Coinage: His authority was \ninternationally recognized in 1229 CE by the Abbasid \nCaliph of Baghdad. Iltutmish also introduced Arabic \ncoinage, laying the foundation for modern currency \nsystems in the region.\nFig. 4.6: Silver tanka of Iltutmish\n• Architectural Patronage: Known as a patron of \narchitecture, he completed the Qutab Minar and \ninitiated the tradition of tomb building in India.\n• Cultural Patronage: Iltutmish patronized \ncontemporary historians like Minhaj-us-Siraj, author \nof “Tabaqat-i-Nasiri”.\n Succession and Legacy:\n• Nomination of Razia: Unconvinced of his sons’ \ncapabilities, Iltutmish took the unprecedented step of \ndeclaring his daughter Razia as his successor, a move \nthat broke traditional norms and set a precedent in \nthe history of the Sultanate.\nIltutmish’s reign was a turning point in the history of the \nDelhi Sultanate, marked by significant military victories, \nadministrative reforms, and cultural advancements, which \nlaid the groundwork for the Sultanate’s future development \nand stability.\nRuknuddin Firuz Shah (c. 1236 CE)\n Brief and Ineffective Reign:\n• Ascension to the Throne: Ruknuddin Firuz Shah, the \neldest son of Iltutmish, was installed as the Sultan by \nthe nobles following his father’s death in 1236 CE. His \nreign was marked by his lack of effective governance \nand over-reliance on others for administration.\n• Government by Proxy: The actual control of the \ngovernment was in the hands of Shah Turkan, \noriginally a Turkish maid, while Ruknuddin engaged \nin extravagant public displays, including rides on \nelephants in Delhi, and distributing gold lavishly.\n• Overthrow and Death: When the governor of \nMultan revolted, Ruknuddin marched to suppress \nit. Seizing this opportunity, his sister Razia, with \nthe support of the amirs of Delhi, usurped the \nthrone. Both Ruknuddin Firuz Shah and Shah Turkan \nwere eventually put to death, marking a swift and \nignominious end to his rule.\nRazia Sultan (c. 1236-1240 CE)\n Trailblazing Female Ruler:\n• Historical Significance: Razia Sultan was the first and \nonly female Muslim ruler of medieval India, known \nfor breaking gender norms and traditional practices."]
