    title: "PDF पृष्ठ 59 — 58 Medieval India • Assertion of Authority: Razia discarded traditional female apparel and purdah, conducting court affairs",
    subtitle: "मूळ PDF मधील पूर्ण पृष्ठीय मजकूर",
    body: ["58 Medieval India \n• Assertion of Authority: Razia discarded traditional \nfemale apparel and purdah, conducting court affairs \nwith her face unveiled. She adopted male attire, rode \nelephants in public, went hunting, and led her army, \nchallenging the entrenched norms of the time.\n• Resistance from Nobility: Her attempts to create \na counter-nobility composed of non-Turks and \nthe appointment of an Abyssinian slave, Malik \nJamaluddin Yaqut, to a high office, invited the wrath \nof the Turkish amirs. Her independent stance and \nrefusal to be a puppet ruler led to revolts by the \nnobility.\n Downfall and Legacy:\n• Rebellions and Imprisonment: Razia faced a serious \nrebellion in Sirhind (Bhatinda) under Altuniya, the \ngovernor. During her campaign against him, she \nwas betrayed, leading to Yaqut’s murder and her \nimprisonment.\n• Return and Defeat: After marrying Altuniya and \nattempting a return to power, Razia was defeated and \nkilled on her way to Delhi by Bahram Shah, another \nson of Iltutmish, in a power struggle that ended her \nhistoric but tumultuous reign.\nIGNITE YOUR MIND\nRazia Sultan broke gender norms but faced opposition \nfrom Turkish nobles for appointing an Abyssinian \nslave to high office. Do you think medieval Indian \nsociety was ready to accept a female ruler?\nBahram Shah (c. 1240-1242 CE)\n Brief and Turbulent Reign: \n• Rise to Power: Following Razia’s fall, Bahram Shah \nascended the throne amidst the growing influence \nand discord among the nobility, particularly the \ngroup known as ‘the Forty’.\n• Struggle for Supremacy: His reign was marked by a \ncontinuous struggle for power between the Sultans \nand the nobles. Initially supported by the nobles, \nBahram’s reign soon devolved into chaos due to \ninfighting and disunity among the ruling elite.\n• Assassination: The unrest and instability of his reign \nculminated in his murder by his own army in 1242 \nCE, reflecting the volatile and uncertain nature of the \nSultanate during this period.\nThe reigns of Ruknuddin, Razia Sultan, and Bahram Shah \nhighlight a period of significant upheaval and change in the \nDelhi Sultanate, marked by challenges to traditional norms, \ninternal power struggles, and the fleeting nature of authority \nduring this medieval era.\nAlauddin Masud Shah (c. 1242-1246 CE)\n Ineffective and Puppet Ruler:\n• Ascension to the Throne: Alauddin Masud Shah, son \nof Ruknuddin Firuz Shah and nephew of Razia Sultan, \nwas chosen as the ruler after the murder of his \npredecessor, Bahram Shah, by the army. His selection \nwas orchestrated by the chiefs seeking to maintain \ntheir influence.\n• Puppet Leadership: Masud Shah’s reign was \ncharacterized by his lack of real power and influence. \nHe was widely seen as a puppet in the hands of the \nchiefs, unable to exert any significant authority over \nthe governance of the Sultanate.\n• Infamous Lifestyle: His rule became infamous \nfor his indulgence in entertainment and wine, \novershadowing any substantial administrative or \npolitical contributions.\n Downfall and Replacement\n• Increasing Discontent: By 1246 CE, discontent grew \namong the chiefs over Masud Shah’s increasing \naspirations for greater power. This led them to \nreplace him with Nasiruddin Mahmud, marking the \nend of his lackluster and ineffective rule.\nNasiruddin Mahmud (c. 1246-1266 CE)\n Reign Dominated by Balban:\n• Inexperienced and Young Ruler: Nasiruddin \nMahmud, the grandson of Iltutmish, was young and \ninexperienced when he ascended the throne. His rise \nwas largely due to the support of Ulugh Khan, later \nknown as Balban, a member of the Chahalgani (a \ngroup of forty powerful military leaders).\n• Balban’s Dominance: Balban assumed the position \nof Naib (deputy or regent) and further solidified \nhis power by marrying his daughter to Nasiruddin. \nThe real authority during Mahmud’s reign lay in the \nhands of Balban.\n Political Intrigues and Balban’s Ascendancy:\n• Conspiracy and Power Struggles: The increasing \nauthority of Balban led to discontent among other \nTurkish chiefs. Around 1250 CE, they ousted Balban \nand replaced him with Imaduddin Raihan, an Indian \nMuslim. This decision was due to the inability of the \nTurkish nobles to agree on a successor from among \nthemselves.\n• Balban’s Return and Strengthening: Balban strategically \nwithdrew from the political forefront, only to return \nwith greater support and power. He was reinstated \nwith even more authority and began to assume royal \ninsignia, signifying his near-sovereign status."]
