    chapter: "04 • The Delhi Sultanate",
    title: "PDF पृष्ठ 60 — The Delhi Sultanate 59  End of Nasiruddin Mahmud’s Reign: • Death and Controversy: Nasiruddin Mahmud died",
    subtitle: "मूळ PDF मधील पूर्ण पृष्ठीय मजकूर",
    body: ["The Delhi Sultanate 59\n End of Nasiruddin Mahmud’s Reign:\n• Death and Controversy: Nasiruddin Mahmud died \nin 1266 CE. According to some historians like Ibn \nBattuta and Isami, there were suspicions that Balban, \nin his quest for power, may have poisoned Nasiruddin \nMahmud to ascend the throne himself.\nNasiruddin Mahmud’s reign, though marked by his nominal \nposition as Sultan, was largely overshadowed by the political \nmachinations and eventual dominance of Balban. This period \nexemplifies the complex power dynamics and intrigues that \ncharacterized the Delhi Sultanate, especially within the \nruling elite and the nobility.\nBalban (c. 1266–1287 CE)\n Architect of the Delhi Sultanate’s Government:\n• Monarchical Power and Divine Right: Balban \nsignificantly enhanced the power and prestige of \nthe monarchy. He believed in the divine right of \nkings, viewing the Sultan as God’s shadow on earth \n(Zil-i-Ilahi) and a recipient of divine grace (Niyyabat￾i-Khudai). He ruled autocratically, elevating the \nSultan’s position as an unchallengeable authority.\n• Elimination of the Forty: Understanding the threat \nposed by the powerful group of nobles known as the \nForty (Chahalgani), Balban systematically dismantled \ntheir influence. He eliminated every member of \nIltutmish’s family and decisively weakened the power \nof the Forty.\n Court Discipline and Policies:\n• Rigorous Court Etiquette: Balban introduced strict \ncourt discipline (Iranian practices), including practices \nlike sijada (prostration), paibos (kissing the Sultan’s \nfeet) and zaminbos (kissing the ground in front of \nthe Sultan) to assert his superiority. He maintained a \nmagnificent court and celebrated the Persian festival \nof Nauroz to display the strength of his government.\n• Austerity and Authority: He stopped displaying \nemotions like laughter and gave up wine to maintain \na serious demeanour in court, reinforcing the awe \nand respect for his position.\n Elimination of Disloyal Nobles: Balban spared only the \nmost obedient nobles, eliminating all others suspected \nof disloyalty, including prominent governors like Haybat \nKhan and Sher Khan.\n Racial Policy and Administration:\n• Exclusion of Non-Turks: Balban was known for \nhis racial bias, favouring Turks and excluding non￾Turks, especially Indian Muslims, from important \nadministrative posts. He set up an efficient spy \nnetwork to monitor the activities of the nobles.\n Military and Administrative Reforms:\n• Dealing with Internal and External Threats: Balban \nfaced numerous challenges: Hostility from Turkish \nchiefs, threats from the Mongols, aspirations of \nindependence by provincial governors, and revolts \nfrom Indian rulers.\n During the tenure of Balban, Mongols reached \nthe banks of the river Indus, and there they were \ndefeated by Balban’s army general Jalaluddin \nKhilji. Many of the defeated Mongols embraced \nIslam and joined Jalaluddin’s Army.\n• Centralized Army and Law Enforcement: He \norganized a strong centralized army to quell internal \ndisturbances and repel Mongol invasions. He also \ndivided key departments for better administration, \nseparating the Finance Department from the Military \nDepartment. \n Diwan-i-Ariz was the separate military department \ncreated by Balban.\n• Restoration of Law and Order: Balban took severe \nactions against robbers and rebels, ensuring safe \ntravel and communication routes. His campaigns in \nregions like Mewat, Awadh, and Katihar were noted \nfor their ruthlessness.\n To avoid the plunder by Mewati Rajputs, Balban \ngot dense forest in and around Delhi cleared. \n• Failures and Limitations: Despite his efforts, Balban \ncould not fully defend Northern India from Mongol \nattacks, and his narrow focus on Turkish nobility \ncaused widespread dissatisfaction.\n Cultural Patronage and Personal Tragedy\n• Patronage of Arts: Balban was a patron of men of \nletters, notably Amir Khusrau, whom he favoured \nwith special attention.\n• Tragic End: The resurgence of Mongol threats in the \nnorthwest led to a military campaign led by his son, \nPrince Mahmud. The prince’s death in battle was a \ndevastating blow to Balban, contributing to his death \nin 1287 CE.\nBalban’s reign was characterized by a policy of ‘Blood and \nIron’ or statist policy, marked by efficient administration, \nsignificant military campaigns, and cultural patronage. \nHowever, his heavy-handed approach and racial policies \nalso planted the seeds of discontent and instability within \nthe Sultanate. His efforts to strengthen the monarchy and \nthe administrative structure left a lasting impact on the Delhi \nSultanate.\nIGNITE YOUR MIND\nDo you think the elimination of non-Turks from \nimportant administrative posts by Balban was a \nprudent policy for the stability of the Sultanate, or \ndid it contribute to social tensions and hinder the \nefficient functioning of the government?"]
