    title: "PDF पृष्ठ 61 — 60 Medieval India Kaiqubad (c. 1287-1290 CE)  Brief and Ineffectual Reign:",
    subtitle: "मूळ PDF मधील पूर्ण पृष्ठीय मजकूर",
    body: ["60 Medieval India \nKaiqubad (c. 1287-1290 CE)\n Brief and Ineffectual Reign:\n• Ascension to Power: Kaiqubad, the grandson of \nBalban, was elevated to the throne by the nobles \nfollowing Balban’s death. His reign marked the \nwaning days of the Slave Dynasty, characterized by \nweak leadership and a lack of effective governance.\n• Replacement by Kaimurs: Kaiqubad’s rule was short￾lived, and he was soon replaced by his son, Kaimurs, \nwho occupied the throne for just over three months. \nThis quick succession indicated the instability \nand power struggles that had engulfed the Delhi \nSultanate.\n Overthrow and the Rise of the Khaljis:\n• Seizure of Power by Jalaluddin Khalji: In a decisive \nmove, Firoz, the Ariz-i-Mumalik (Minister of War), \nwho later adopted the title of Jalaluddin Khalji, \nmurdered Kaimurs and seized the throne. This bold \naction was supported by a group of Khalji nobles.\n• Establishment of the Khalji Dynasty: The ascension \nof Jalaluddin Khalji marked a significant shift in the \nhistory of the Delhi Sultanate. This event is often \nreferred to as the ‘dynastic revolution’ of 1290 CE. \nIt brought an end to the so-called Slave Dynasty and \nushered in the era of the Khaljis.\n Significance of the Dynastic Change:\n• End of an Era: Kaiqubad’s ineffective reign and the \nsubsequent rise of Jalaluddin Khalji symbolized the \nend of the first phase of Muslim rule in India under \nthe Slave Dynasty. The transition to the Khalji dynasty \nrepresented a shift in power dynamics and a new \napproach to governance and administration within \nthe Sultanate.\n Emergence of New Leadership: The Khaljis, unlike their \npredecessors, were not of Turkish origin but hailed \nfrom the Afghan Khalji tribe. This change in leadership \nindicated a broader inclusivity in the political landscape \nof the Delhi Sultanate, moving away from the previously \ndominant Turkish aristocracy.\nThe period marked by Kaiqubad’s brief reign and the rise \nof the Khaljis was a turning point in the history of the Delhi \nSultanate, indicating both the decline of the established \norder and the beginning of a new era under the Khalji \ndynasty.\nKhalji Dynasty (c. 1290-1320 CE)\nThe Khalji dynasty, marking a significant shift in the Delhi \nSultanate’s history, ruled from around 1290 to 1320 CE. This \nera represented a departure from the Turkish dominance of \nthe Slave Dynasty, as the Khaljis were of Afghan origin. The \ndynasty is known for its expansionist policies, administrative \nreforms, and a more inclusive approach towards the non￾Muslim population of India. The rulers of the Khalji dynasty, \nnotably Jalaluddin Khalji and his nephew Alauddin Khalji, \nwere instrumental in extending the Sultanate’s boundaries \nand consolidating its power.\nFig. 4.7: Khalji Dynasty (1290-1320)\nFig. 4.8: The Rulers of Khalji Dynasty"]
