    chapter: "04 • The Delhi Sultanate",
    title: "PDF पृष्ठ 63 — 62 Medieval India  At the most inopportune moment for Alauddin Khilji, the Mongols launched an assault while he was",
    subtitle: "मूळ PDF मधील पूर्ण पृष्ठीय मजकूर",
    body: ["62 Medieval India \n At the most inopportune moment for Alauddin \nKhilji, the Mongols launched an assault while he was \nengaged in besieging Chittor. The Mongol forces, led \nby Targhi with a swift-moving army of 12,000, caught \nKhilji off guard. Due to the rapid nature of the attack, \nseveral governors were unable to dispatch their \ntroops to Delhi promptly.\n Consequently, Alauddin Khilji had no choice but to \nretreat to Siri for approximately two months. The \nMongols not only targeted the surrounding regions \nbut also conducted raids and looting within the city \nof Delhi itself.\n Military Conquests:\n• Gujarat: Alauddin began his territorial expansion by \ninvading Gujarat in 1299 CE, attracted by its riches \nand strategic importance. The conquest resulted in \nenormous booty and the acquisition of Malik Kafur, \na slave who later became a trusted confidante of \nAlauddin Khalji.\n• Rajputana: Alauddin’s campaign in Rajputana focused \non Ranthambore and Chittor, both of which were \ncaptured amidst fierce resistance and led to instances \nof Jauhar (self-immolation) by Rajput women.\n• Conquest of Deccan and South India: Under Malik \nKafur, Alauddin’s forces ventured into the Deccan \nand South India, subjugating kingdoms like Yadavas\n(Devagiri), Kakatiyas (Warangal), the Hoysalas \n(Dwarsamudram), and the Pandyas (Malbaar), \namassing vast wealth.\n Kakatiya ruler Pratap Rudra gift Koh-i-noor\ndiamond to Alauddin Khilji.\n Administrative and Cultural Contributions:\n• Land Revenue and Secular Kingship: Alauddin \nwas the first Sultan to fix land revenue in cash and \nadopted a secular approach to kingship, famously \nproclaiming, “Kingship knows no Kinship.”\n• Non-Turkish Inclusion and Patronage: He included \nnon-Turks in his service and patronized poets like \nAmir Khusrau, earning him the title of Tuti-i-Hind\n(Parrot of India).\nIGNITE YOUR MIND\nAlauddin Khalji established a large standing army and \ncavalry force and regulated market and commodity \nprices. Do you think military strength or economic \nstability was crucial for the longevity of medieval \nkingdoms?\n• Architectural Achievements: Alauddin constructed \nnotable structures like Alai Darwaza, Hauz Khas, \nMahal Hazaar Satoon, Jamait Khana Mosque, Alai \nMinar, and established the new capital at Siri.\n• Refutation of Caliph’s Suzerainty: He refuted the \nsuzerainty of the Caliph and did not allow any \nreligious authority to influence his state policies.\nQutbuddin Mubarak Shah (c. 1316–1320 CE)\n Turbulent Succession and Rule:\n• Post-Alauddin’s Death: The Delhi Sultanate faced \na period of confusion and instability following the \ndeath of Alauddin Khalji. Malik Kafur briefly seized \nthe throne but was soon deposed.\n• Ascension to Power: Qutbuddin Mubarak Shah, \none of Alauddin’s sons who evaded Malik Kafur’s \nattempts to eliminate potential rivals, ascended the \nthrone amidst this turmoil.\n• Self-Declaration as Caliph: Mubarak Shah declared \nhimself Caliph, adopting the title Al Wasiq Billah, \nsignifying his assertion of religious as well as political \nauthority.\n Policies and Governance\n• Reversal of Alauddin’s Policies: He cancelled many of \nthe harsh regulations imposed by his father, Alauddin \nKhalji, possibly in an attempt to ease the stringent \nadministrative measures and gain popular support.\n• Handling of Rebellions: Despite being able to \nsuppress several rebellions during his reign, including \nthose in Devagiri (Deogir), Mubarak Shah struggled \nto maintain smooth administration and control over \nthe Sultanate.\n Downfall and Assassination\n• Murder and Succession: Qutbuddin Mubarak Shah’s \nreign came to an abrupt end when he was murdered. \nFollowing his assassination, Nasiruddin Khusrau Shah \nseized the throne.\nNasiruddin Khusrau Shah (c. 1320 CE)\n Brief and Final Khalji Ruler:\n• Regicide and Ascension: Nasiruddin Khusrau Shah, \nwho was responsible for killing Mubarak Shah, \nascended to the throne but his reign was short-lived.\n• Unique Historical Position: Khusrau Shah holds the \ndistinction of being the only Hindu convert to sit on \nthe throne of the Delhi Sultanate.\n Overthrow and Transition of Power:\n• End of Khalji Dynasty: His rule was quickly ended by \na group of dissatisfied officers led by Ghazi Malik, the \ngovernor of Dipalpur.\n• Rise of the Tughlaq Dynasty: Ghazi Malik, upon killing \nKhusrau Shah, ascended the throne of Delhi with the \ntitle of Ghiyasuddin Tughlaq in c. 1320 CE, marking \nthe end of the Khalji dynasty and the beginning of \nthe Tughlaq era."]
