    title: "PDF पृष्ठ 64 — The Delhi Sultanate 63 Administration of Alauddin Khalji Alauddin Khalji, one of the most powerful rulers of the Delhi",
    subtitle: "मूळ PDF मधील पूर्ण पृष्ठीय मजकूर",
    body: ["The Delhi Sultanate 63\nAdministration of Alauddin Khalji\nAlauddin Khalji, one of the most powerful rulers of the Delhi \nSultanate, introduced several revolutionary administrative \nreforms, focusing particularly on military, market, and land \nrevenue systems. These reforms not only strengthened his \nrule but also laid the foundation for future administrative \npractices in India.\n Military Reforms:\n• Permanent Standing Army: Alauddin was the first \nSultan to establish a large permanent standing army, \nwhich was paid in cash from the royal treasury. This \nmove was pivotal in maintaining a strong, centralized \ncontrol over his vast empire.\n• Horse Import and Recruitment: He imported horses \nand recruited a massive cavalry force. Historian \nFerishta notes that Alauddin recruited about 475,000 \ncavalrymen, underscoring his emphasis on a powerful \nmilitary.\n• Chehra/Huliya and Dagh System: An innovative \nsystem called Chehra/Huliya (detailed description \nof each soldier) and Dagh (branding of horses) was \nintroduced. These measures ensured maximum \nefficiency and accountability in the army, with regular \nreviews and inspections.\n Market Reforms:\n• Market Regulation and Price Control: Alauddin \nis particularly noted for his measures to control \nmarkets and regulate prices. To sustain his large army \nand administrative machinery, he fixed the prices of \nessential commodities.\n• Establishment of Separate Markets: Four distinct \nmarkets were established in Delhi for different \ncommodities: grain, cloth and groceries, horses, \nslaves, and cattle, and miscellaneous items.\n• Strict Enforcement and Surveillance: Each market \nwas under a superintendent (Shahna-i-Mandi), \nassisted by intelligence officers. Additionally, \nindependent reports from barids (intelligence \nofficers) and munhiyans (secret spies) ensured \nadherence to regulations.\n• Price Checks and Punishments: The Sultan personally \nmonitored market prices, including sending slave boys \nto check prices. Violations like overcharging, hoarding, or \nusing false measures were met with harsh punishments \nsuch as fines, imprisonment, and expulsion.\n Land Revenue Administration:\n• Land Measurement and Taxation: Alauddin was \nthe first Sultan to order land measurement for tax \nassessment. Land revenue was collected in cash, \nwhich allowed the Sultan to pay his soldiers similarly. \nThis system was comprehensive, even big landlords \ncouldn’t evade tax.\n He abolished the small Iqta system while \ncontinuing with large Iqta.\n• Standardization of Measurement and Tax Rates: The \ncultivated land was measured, and land revenue was \nfixed accordingly, with Biswa as the standard unit. \nThe state demand was set at one-fifth of the produce, \nalong with house tax (Grahi) and pasture tax (Chari).\n• Abolishment of Headman’s Cess: The Kismat-i-Khuti \n(Headman’s cess) was abolished, further streamlining \nthe revenue system.\n• Centralization and Confiscation of Endowments: \nAlauddin centralized administration and confiscated \nreligious endowments and free lands (Inam and \nWaqf), indicating his focus on strengthening state \ncontrol over resources.\n Impact and Legacy:\n• Alauddin Khalji’s administrative reforms had a lasting \nimpact, providing a basis for future reforms by rulers \nlike Sher Shah and Akbar. \n• His approach to administration was characterized \nby centralization, efficiency, and strict enforcement, \nsignificantly enhancing the Sultanate’s military \nstrength, economic stability, and administrative \ncapabilities. \nTughlaq Dynasty (c. 1320–1414 CE)/\nDynasty of Qaraunah Turks\nThe Tughlaq Dynasty, known for its rulers of Qaraunah Turk \norigin, ruled the Delhi Sultanate from approximately 1320 to \n1414 CE. This period is marked by ambitious administrative \nreforms, extensive territorial expansion, and unique yet \noften unfruitful experiments. The Tughlaqs, succeeding the \nKhalji Dynasty, are known for their complex and sometimes \nerratic policies, which had profound effects on the political \nand social structure of the Sultanate.\nGhiyasuddin Tughlaq (c. 1320–1324 CE)\n Founder of the Dynasty:\n• First Sultan with the Title of Ghazi: Ghiyasuddin \nTughlaq was the founder of the Tughlaq dynasty and \nthe first Sultan to adopt the title of Ghazi, meaning \n“Slayer of Infidels.”\n• Irrigation and Fortification: He was the first Sultan \nto initiate irrigation works, showcasing his interest \nin agricultural development. He also built the strong \nfort of Tughlaqabad near Delhi, emphasizing military \nstrength and defense.\n• Military Campaigns: Ghiyasuddin dispatched his son, \nJauna Khan (later known as Muhammad bin Tughlaq), to \nreassert control over regions like Warangal and Madurai.\n Death and Speculations:\n• Mysterious Demise: His death resulted from the \ncollapse of a wooden structure. Some scholars, like \nIbn Batuta, speculate that his death was an act of \nsabotage orchestrated by his son, Jauna Khan. He \nalso had a troubled relationship with the Sufi saint, \nSheikh Nizamuddin Auliya."]
