    id: "m4-pdf-page-66",
    chapter: "04 • The Delhi Sultanate",
    title: "PDF पृष्ठ 66 — The Delhi Sultanate 65 Muhammad Bin Tughlaq/Jauna Khan (c. 1324–1351 CE)",
    subtitle: "मूळ PDF मधील पूर्ण पृष्ठीय मजकूर",
    body: ["The Delhi Sultanate 65\nMuhammad Bin Tughlaq/Jauna Khan \n(c. 1324–1351 CE)\n Reign Characterized by Ambitious Projects:\n• An ill-fated Idealist: Muhammad bin Tughlaq is often \nregarded as an ill-fated idealist for his ambitious yet \nfailed experiments, which were considered far ahead \nof their time.\n• Five Notable Experiments: His reign is marked \nby five major experiments or campaigns, which \nincluded the transfer of the capital to Devagiri, an \naborted expedition to occupy Khurasan and Iraq, \nharsh taxation in the Doab region during a famine, \nintroduction of token currency, and the Qarachil\nexpedition.\n• Cultural and Educational Prowess: He was the only \nDelhi Sultan with comprehensive literary, religious, \nand philosophical education, proficient in Arabic and \nPersian, and skilled in various fields like philosophy, \nastronomy, logic, and mathematics.\n Diplomacy, Reforms, and Revolts:\n• Religious Tolerance and Diplomatic Relations: \nMuhammad bin Tughlaq was known for his religious \ntolerance and maintained diplomatic relations with \ndistant countries like Egypt, China, and Iran.\n• Agricultural Reforms: He established the department \nof Agriculture, Diwan-i-amir-kohi, and launched \nschemes like takkavi-agricultural loans for farmers.\n• Architectural Contributions: He built the fort of \nAdilabad and the city of Jahanpanah.\n• Independent Southern States: During his reign, the \nsouthern part of India became independent, leading \nto the emergence of the Vijayanagar Kingdom, the \nBahmani Kingdom, and the Sultanate of Madurai.\n• Decline and Death: His last days were marred \nby widespread revolts and the decline of central \nauthority. He died in c. 1351 CE, with chroniclers \ndescribing his reign as a mix of opposites and the \nbeginning of the decline of the Tughlaq Dynasty.\nIGNITE YOUR MIND\nMuhammad Bin Tughlaq is known for his experimental \nreforms that were ambitious but often unsuccessful. \nDo you think his approach of experimentation should \nbe commended for being ahead of time or criticized \nfor impracticality?\nFiroz Shah Tughlaq (c. 1351–1388 CE)\n Ascension and Policies:\n• Chosen by Nobles: Firoz Shah Tughlaq had the unique \ndistinction of being chosen as Sultan by the nobles, \ninfluencing his policies to appease them, the army, \nand theologians.\n• Appointment of Wazir: He appointed Khan-i￾Jahan Maqbal, a Telugu Brahmin convert, as Wazir\n(prime minister), who played a crucial role in the \nadministration and maintaining the prestige of the \nSultanate.\n Military and Political Campaigns:\n• Focus on Northern India: Firoz Shah focused on \nsafeguarding his authority over North India rather than \nreasserting control over the Deccan and South India. His \ntwo campaigns to Bengal were unsuccessful, leading to \nBengal’s independence from the Delhi Sultanate.\n• Campaigns in Orissa and Other Regions: He led \na campaign against Jajnagar (modern Orissa), \nreturning with substantial booty from temples like \nPuri Jagannath. He also campaigned in Nagarkot and \nThatta (Sindh region), suppressing a rebellion and \ncollecting tributes.\n Religious and Social Policies\n• Adherence to Islamic Law: Firoz Shah strictly \nfollowed Islamic law in administration. He imposed \nJiziya on non-Muslims and treated Hindus as second￾grade citizens. He abolished taxes not sanctioned \nby Islamic law and imposed restrictions on Muslim \nwomen visiting saints’ graves.\n• Persecution of Diverse Sects: Firoz Shah persecuted \nvarious heretical Muslim sects, including Shia \nMuslims and Sufis.\n• Regard for the Caliph of Egypt: He maintained a high \nregard for the Caliph of Egypt, considering himself as \nhis deputy, and received robes of honour from him.\n Economic and Administrative Reforms\n• Introduction of New Taxes and Canals: Firoz Shah \nintroduced:\n Sharb (Irrigation Tax), Kharaj (Land Tax), Zakat (Tax \non income of Muslims for propogation of Islam). \n He built numerous canals, including significant \nones like Sirsa to Hansi and Sutlej to Hansi. He \nalso established a separate department for public \nworks.\n• Hereditary Iqtadari System: He revived and made \nthe Iqtadari system hereditary, where soldiers were \ngiven land assignments (jagir system), and their \nrecruitment was also made hereditary.\n• Development of Karkhanas: He developed royal \nfactories (karkhanas) employing thousands of slaves \nunder the Diwan-i-Bandagan (department of slaves).\n• Urban Development and Repairs: Firoz Shah built \naround 300 new towns, including Firozabad near Red \nFort in Delhi, Fatehabad, Firojshah Kotla, Hisar, and \nJaunpur. He repaired and maintained old monuments \nlike the Jama Masjid and the Qutab Minar.\n Cultural and Scholarly Patronage:\n• Scholarly Patronage: He patronized scholars like \nBarani and Khwajja Abdul Malik Islami and wrote \n“Futuhat-e-Firozshahi” ."]
