    subtitle: "मूळ PDF मधील पूर्ण पृष्ठीय मजकूर",
    body: ["70 Medieval India \nImpact and Legacy\n Influence on Subsequent Empires: The Sultanate’s \nadministrative system laid the groundwork for future \nempires, particularly the Mughals. Its blend of centralized \nauthority with a structured bureaucratic system sets a \nprecedent for efficient governance.\nOfficers under Delhi Sultanate\nOfficer Role & Responsibilities\nAmil Revenue Officer\nBarid Intelligence agents\nKhwaja Accounts Officer\nKotwal Head of city law and order\nMuhatasib Head of municipality law and order\nMuftis Expounder of law\nProvincial Government in the Sultanate Period\nIqtas (Province)\nShiqs (District)\npargana -(Sub-Districts) Amil\nShiqdar\nMuqtis or Walis(Governor)\nVillage Muqaddam or Chaudhri\nFig. 4.16: The Provincial Government\n Structure and Management\n• Iqtas System: The provinces, called iqtas, were initially \nmanaged by nobles. Governors, known as muqtis or \nwalis, maintained law and order and collected land \nrevenue.\n• Administrative Divisions: Provinces were divided \ninto shiqs, managed by shiqdars, and further into \nparganas, comprising several villages and headed by \namils. Villages, the basic administrative units, were \ngrouped into chaurasis.\n• Village Administration: The village headman, \nknown as muqaddam or chaudhri, and the village \naccountant, patwari, were key figures in local \nadministration.\nECONOMY AND LAND REVENUE\n Land Classification: Land was categorized as Iqta land \n(assigned to officials), Khalisa land (under direct Sultan’s \ncontrol; revenue used for Sultan’s personal expenses), \nand Inam land (assigned to religious leaders/institutions).\n Agricultural Reforms: Sultans like Muhammad bin \nTughlaq and Firoz Tughlaq enhanced agricultural \nproduction through irrigation and takkavi loans. They \nencouraged the cultivation of superior crops and \nhorticulture. Muhammad bin Tughlaq established \nDiwan-i-Kohi, a separate agricultural department.\n Peasantry and Taxation: Peasants, who often led a \nsubsistence existence, paid a significant portion of their \nproduce sometimes upto half of the entire prouduce, \nas land revenue. Frequent famines exacerbated their \nhardships.\n Urbanization and Trade: Urbanization accelerated during \nthe Sultanate, with cities like Delhi, Lahore, Multan, and \nothers growing significantly. \n• Trade flourished, with India exporting textiles, gold, \nand silver work to West Asia and importing luxury \ngoods. \n• Inland trade was dominated by Gujarati, Marwari, \nand Muslim Bohra merchants.\n Infrastructure Development: The constructionn of \nroads and sarais (rest houses) facilitated transport and \ncommunication. The paper industry and other crafts like \nleather-making, metal-crafts, and carpet-weaving also \nflourished.\nAraghatta/Persian Wheel\n This device made its entry into India with the \nestablishment of the Delhi Sultanate.\n In Sanskrit, the word Araghatta has been used in \nancient texts to describe the Persian Wheel.\n The ‘ara-ghatta’ comes from the combination of the \nwords ‘ara’ meaning spoke and ‘ghatta’ meaning pot.\n There is evidence to argue that this system of lifting \nwater from open wells was probably invented in India \nof the past. It finds mention in the Panchatantra (3rd \nCentury BCE) and the Rajatarangini (12th century CE) \nas the ‘cakka-vattakka’ or the ‘ghati yantra’.\n The Araghattikka or arahattiyanara describes the \nperson or animal working the Araghatta and this \ndescription was extensively used in the twelfth \ncentury.\n Usually, men, bullocks, elephants or camels did the \njob of moving laterally to lift water by this system of \nAraghatta.\n The Persian wheel is a mechanical water lifting device \noperated usually by draught animals like bullocks, \nbuffaloes, or camels. It is used to lift water from water \nsources typically open wells.\n It uses the system of gearing to convert the circular \nmotion of the animal into the vertical motion of the \nwheel on spokes."]
