    title: "PDF पृष्ठ 72 — The Delhi Sultanate 71 COINAGE AND TECHNOLOGICAL ADVANCEMENTS",
    subtitle: "मूळ PDF मधील पूर्ण पृष्ठीय मजकूर",
    body: ["The Delhi Sultanate 71\nCOINAGE AND TECHNOLOGICAL \nADVANCEMENTS\n Coinage System: Iltutmish issued silver tankas, which \nwere subdivided into jitals during the Khalji and Tughlaq \nrules. Gold coins gained popularity during Alauddin \nKhalji’s reign, and Muhammad bin Tughlaq experimented \nwith token currency along with issuing gold and silver \ncoins.\n Crafts and Techniques: The Turks introduced the iron \nstirrup, armour for horses and riders, improvement of \nthe rahat (Persian wheel), paper-making, glass-making, \nthe spinning wheel, and an improved loom. They also \nused superior mortar for constructing buildings based on \narches and domes.\nSOCIAL SYSTEM DURING \nTHE SULTANATE PERIOD\n Hindu Society:\n• Caste System: The traditional Hindu caste system, \nwith Brahmins at the upper strata, continued with \nlittle change during the Sultanate period.\n• Status of Women: The subservient position of \nwomen in society persisted. Practices like Sati (self￾immolation of a widow on her husband’s funeral \npyre) were prevalent, as observed by travellers like \nIbn Batuta.\n• Purdah System: The introduction of the purdah \nsystem by Arabs and Turks led to the seclusion of \nwomen, especially among the upper Hindu classes in \nNorth India. The practice of wearing purdah became \nwidespread.\n Muslim Society:\n• Ethnic and Racial Divisions: The Muslim society was \ndivided into various groups - Turks, Iranians, Afghans, \nand Indian Muslims. These groups remained \nexclusive, with little intermarriage.\n• Status of Hindu Converts: Converts from Hindu lower \ncastes were often not accorded equal respect within \nthe Muslim society.\n• Nobility and Government Positions: Muslim nobles \noccupied high offices, and Hindu nobles rarely held \nsignificant government positions.\n• Jizya Tax: Hindus were considered ‘zimmis’\n(protected people) and were required to pay the jizya \ntax. Initially part of the land tax, Firoz Tughlaq made \njizya a separate tax, also levied on Brahmins.\n Slavery:\n• Prevalence of Slavery: Slavery, existing since ancient \ntimes in India, flourished during the Sultanate \nperiod. Slave markets were common, and slaves \nwere employed in various capacities, from household \nchores to skilled professions.\n• Large Slave Populations: Rulers like Firoz Shah \nTughlaq had extensive numbers of slaves, with \nestimates of around 180,000 in his reign.\nIGNITE YOUR MIND\nDo you think the socioeconomic conditions of the \npeasantry during the Sultanate period were more \ninfluenced by land revenue policies or external \nfactors such as famines?\nART AND ARCHITECTURE DURING \nTHE DELHI SULTANATE PERIOD\nThe art and architecture of the Delhi Sultanate period \nwere significantly distinct from the traditional Indian style, \nmarking a blend of Islamic and indigenous elements.\n Architectural Features and Innovations:\n• Turkish Influence: The Turks introduced architectural \nfeatures like arches, domes, lofty towers (minarets),\nand decorations using Arabic script, blending them \nwith the skills of Indian stone-cutters.\n• Use of Colored Stones: They added colour to their \nbuildings by using materials like marbles, red and \nyellow sandstones, enhancing the visual appeal of \nthe structures.\n• Conversion of Temples: \nInitially, many Hindu \ntemples were converted \ninto mosques. An \nexample is the Quwwat￾ul-Islam mosque near \nQutub Minar in Delhi, \nwhich was formerly a \nVishnu temple.\n• Construction of New \nStructures: Subsequently, the rulers began constructing \nnew structures, with a policy allowing the repair of old \ntemples but forbidding new Hindu places of worship.\n Notable Architectural Works:\n• Qutub Minar: The most \nmagnificent building \nof the 13th century, \nthe Qutub Minar, was \nfounded by Aibak and \ncompleted by Iltutmish. \nDedicated to Sufi saint \nKhwaja Qutbuddin \nBakthiyar Kaki, it \nstands at 72.5 meters with balconies projecting \nfrom the main structure, showcasing advanced \narchitectural skills. Alauddin Khalji added the \nAlai Darwaza, an entrance with a scientifically \nconstructed dome.\nFig. 4.17: Quwwat Ul \nIslam Mosque \nFig. 4.18: Qutub Minar"]
