    title: "PDF पृष्ठ 73 — 72 Medieval India • Tughlaq Architecture: Buildings from the Tughlaq period combined the use of arch and dome and",
    subtitle: "मूळ PDF मधील पूर्ण पृष्ठीय मजकूर",
    body: ["72 Medieval India \n• Tughlaq Architecture: Buildings from the Tughlaq \nperiod combined the use of arch and dome and \npredominantly used grey-coloured stones. The \nTughlaqabad palace complex and the tomb of \nGhyasuddin Tughlaq, built on a high platform, are \nnotable examples.\n• Kotla Fort and Lodhi Gardens: The Kotla Fort in \nDelhi was a creation of Firoz Tughlaq, and the Lodhi \nGardens represent the architectural style of the \nLodhi dynasty.\nMusic During the Delhi Sultanate Period\nThe Delhi Sultanate period was marked by significant \ncontributions to the development of Indian music, with the \nintroduction of new instruments and styles.\nNew Instruments and Styles:\n Sarangi and Rabab: New musical instruments like the \nsarangi and rabab were introduced during this period, \nenriching the musical landscape of India.\n Amir Khusrau’s Contributions: Amir Khusrau, a notable \nmusician of this era, introduced new ragas like Ghora and\nSanam. He also developed qawwalis, a new style of light \nmusic, by blending Hindu and Iranian musical systems. \nThe invention of the sitar is also attributed to him.\n Translations and Sufi Influences: The Indian classical \nwork, Ragadarpan, was translated into Persian under \nFiroz Tughlaq’s reign. Sufi saint Pir Bhodan was another \nprominent musician of this period. Raja Man Singh \nof Gwalior, a great music enthusiast, encouraged the \ncomposition of the musical work ‘Man Kautuhal’.\nLiterature During The Delhi Sultanate \nPeriod\n Patronage of Learning and Literature:\n• Love for Arabic and Persian Literature: The Delhi \nSultans had a deep appreciation for Arabic and Persian \nliterature, which is why, they attracted learned men \nfrom Persia and encouraging the Persian language.\n• Historical Writings: The writing of history was \nparticularly encouraged. Notable historians included \nHasan Nizami, Minhaj-us-Siraj, Ziauddin Barani, and\nShams Siraj Afif. Barani’s ‘Tarikh-i-Firoz Shahi’ details \nthe history of the Tughlaq dynasty, while Minhaj-us￾Siraj authored ‘Tabaqat-i-Nasari’.\n Amir Khusrau and His Contributions:\n• Prominent Persian Writer: Amir Khusrau was the most \nrenowned Persian writer of this period, known for his \npoems and the creation of the ‘Sabaq-i-Hind’ or the \nIndian style of Persian poetry. He also composed Hindi \nverses.\n• Literary Works: His notable works include ‘Khazain￾ul-Futuh’ about Alauddin’s conquests and ‘Tughlaq \nNama’ detailing the rise of Ghiyasuddin Tughlaq.\n Role of Sanskrit and Persian:\n• Link Languages: Sanskrit and Persian served as link \nlanguages in the Sultanate. Zia Nakshabi was the first \nto translate Sanskrit stories into Persian, with works \nlike ‘Tutu Nama’ gaining popularity and translations \ninto various languages.\n• Regional Language Development: Regional languages \nlike Bengali and Hindi also developed during this \nperiod. Chand Bardai was a renowned Hindi poet, \nand Nusrat Shah patronized the translation of the \nMahabharata into Bengali.\nIGNITE YOUR MIND\nEvaluate the architectural legacy of the Delhi \nSultanate, considering the blend of Islamic and \nindigenous elements. To what extent did their \ninnovations, like the use of arches, domes, and \ncolored stones, influence the development of \nsubsequent architectural styles in India?\nCONCLUSION\nThe Delhi Sultanate period, spanning from the early 13th to \nthe mid-16th century, marked a transformative era in Indian \nhistory, characterized by significant political, social, and cultural \ndevelopments. The Sultanate’s establishment led to the \nevolution of a complex administrative system, with a powerful \ncentral authority supported by a well-structured bureaucracy. \nThe social fabric of the time reflected traditional Hindu \ncustoms alongside the influence of Islamic culture, evident in \nthe caste system, the status of women, and the division within \nthe Muslim society. The period was also remarkable for its \narchitectural innovations, introducing a unique blend of Islamic \nand indigenous styles, as seen in the iconic Qutub Minar and \nthe use of arches and domes. Music and literature flourished \nunder the Sultanate, with the introduction of new instruments, \nstyles, and significant Persian literary contributions by figures \nlike Amir Khusrau. The administrative, social, and cultural \nlegacies of the Delhi Sultanate profoundly impacted subsequent \nIndian kingdoms and empires, laying the groundwork for future \ndevelopments in these realms."]
